        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Ellipticals</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. The picks were made from manufacturer specification
            tables, retailer listings, warranty terms and independent coverage, all checked on 16
            October 2026. Where we say what reviewers or owners report, it is because we found that
            reporting, and we say so.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>Currently sold:</strong> each machine had to be on
            sale under that name on its maker's own store on 16 October 2026; the NordicTrack X16
            and SOLE E95S are sold through their brands' stores rather than on Amazon.
            <strong>Agreement:</strong> stride length, user capacity, flywheel weight and footprint
            had to match between the maker and a source that does not make the product. Where they
            did not, the figure was left out of the card. <strong>A real warranty:</strong> every pick
            carries a published frame warranty of at least ten years, and the brand has its own
            support route. <strong>No safety action:</strong> we searched the US Consumer Product
            Safety Commission recall database by every brand on the page and by product type.
          </p>
          <p>
            That safety search found nothing current against any machine listed here, and no elliptical recall of any brand since 2019. It did turn up
            old elliptical recalls from two of the brands — Horizon Fitness elliptical pedals in 2010
            (models EX-58, EX-68, EX-78 and others, not the EX-59) and the Schwinn 460 elliptical
            in 2011, sold under the previous owner. NordicTrack's parent company recalled a NordicTrack
            rowing machine for a fire hazard in October 2025; that recall does not cover its
            ellipticals. If you buy any of these brands used, check the serial number against the
            CPSC database before you pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some machines are missing on purpose. The Schwinn 430, which would have been our
            pick for a manual incline ramp, showed as out of stock on Schwinn's own store when we
            checked. The NordicTrack AirGlide 16 is on sale, but NordicTrack's product data for it
            was mixed in with other models' figures on the pages we read, and we could not confirm its
            numbers independently. The SOLE E25 and E20 are both sound and both currently sold;
            they appear in the text below as alternatives rather than cards. Stepper-style
            machines such as BowFlex's Max Trainers are not ellipticals in the usual sense, so they are
            covered in our
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cardio machines guide</a>
            instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stride-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Stride Length and Your Height</h2>
<p>
            Stride length is the distance the pedals travel from front to back, and it is the
            single figure most likely to decide whether an elliptical feels natural or cramped. Too
            short and you shuffle, with your knees and hips working through a smaller range than
            walking asks of them; owners of short-stride machines commonly describe it as feeling
            like pedalling on the spot. Too long is rarely a problem for a tall user, but a shorter
            user can find a very long stride a reach.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A rough guide, not a rule</h3>
          <p>
            There is no industry standard linking height to stride. The rule of thumb repeated by
            retailers and reviewers runs roughly like this: an 18-inch stride suits most users up
            to average height, 20 inches suits most adults including many taller ones, and users
            over about six feet tend to prefer 21 inches or more. Treat that as a starting point,
            not a prescription; leg length, how you like to move and whether you mostly walk or
            push hard all change it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>18 inches</strong> is the compact standard. The Horizon EX-59 and Schwinn
            411 both use it, and both are aimed at smaller rooms and budgets. Fine for most
            people of average height and below; a tall user may find it short.</li>
            <li><strong>20 inches</strong> is the common full-size figure, on the SOLE E35 and the
            Schwinn 490. It is the safe choice if you do not know who will use the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Adjustable strides</strong> solve the household problem. The SOLE E95S
            lets each user set anywhere from 18 to 24 inches. The NordicTrack X16 takes a different
            approach: its stride adjusts automatically up to 32 inches as you move, which is
            closer to a walking or running motion than a fixed loop.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Try before you buy, if you can</h3>
          <p>
            The best test is ten minutes on the machine, or on one with the same stride. If that is
            not possible, measure your comfortable walking step indoors and compare: a stride much
            shorter than your natural step will feel like it. Pedal spacing — the gap between the
            pedals, sometimes called the Q-factor — matters too; Horizon makes a point of a narrow
            pedal spacing on the EX-59, and SOLE publishes the figure for the E95S. Narrower is
            generally closer to the way your feet track when you walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bear in mind who else will use it. An elliptical bought for one person tends to become
            the household's machine. If the users range from five feet to six and a half, an
            adjustable stride is worth more than a bigger screen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drive-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Front Drive, Rear Drive and Centre Drive</h2>
<p>
            The drive is where the flywheel sits, and it shapes the motion, the footprint and the
            feel. Listings mention it often without explaining what it changes, so here is the
            practical difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Front drive.</strong> The flywheel is at the front, under or ahead of the
            console, and the pedal arms run back on rails or wheels. The motion tends to feel more
            like climbing or walking uphill, with a more upright, slightly forward-leaning posture.
            Front-drive machines are usually the cheapest to build well, and they put the moving parts
            where you can reach them. Most current home ellipticals are front drive: SOLE describes
            the E35, E95S, E25 and E20 that way, and reviewers describe the Horizon EX-59 as front
            drive too. The rails need wiping down, and the wheels that run on them are the usual
            source of the clicking or thumping noises owners report as machines age.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rear drive.</strong> The flywheel sits behind you. The motion is generally
            described as flatter and closer to a walking stride, and rear-drive machines are often
            longer front to back. They were once the gym standard and are now less common in
            home models. If you have used gym ellipticals for years and liked the feel, it is worth
            trying a rear-drive model before assuming a front-drive home machine will feel the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Centre drive.</strong> The flywheel sits beneath you, between the pedals. The
            appeal is a shorter machine — the frame does not need to extend in front of or behind
            the pedals — and a very upright posture. The trade-offs are typically a shorter stride
            and a slightly higher step-on height. Centre drive is a sensible thing to look for if
            length is your problem, but check the stride figure carefully.
          </p>
          <p>
            Drive type matters less than it used to, and much less than stride length and frame
            quality. A well-made front-drive machine with a 20-inch stride will suit more people
            than a poorly made rear-drive one. Use the drive type to understand the footprint and
            the feel, not as a ranking. Two of our picks — the Schwinn models and the NordicTrack X16
            — are not described by drive type on the makers' pages we read, so we have not classed
            them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ceiling-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Ceiling Height: Check the Top of the Stride</h2>
<p>
            This is the measurement people forget, and the one that sends machines back. On an
            elliptical you stand on pedals that rise as they travel. At the top of the stride your
            head is your full height plus the pedal height above the floor, and on a machine with an
            incline ramp that pedal height goes up again when the ramp is raised.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to work it out</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Find the maker's minimum ceiling height or maximum pedal height. Some publish it
            plainly — Schwinn's spec sheets give a minimum ceiling as your height plus a figure for
            each model. Others bury it in the owner's manual.</li>
            <li>Add your height, with the trainers you will wear.</li>
            <li>Add a few inches of headroom, because you rise slightly with each step and nobody
            wants to train with their hair brushing the plaster.</li>
            <li>Compare with the lowest point of the ceiling where the machine will stand —
            beams, ducts and light fittings included.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A worked example: Schwinn lists the 411's minimum ceiling as user height plus 17 inches,
            and an independent review gives the same 17-inch pedal height. A six-foot user
            therefore needs at least 89 inches before headroom. Under a standard eight-foot ceiling
            that is fine; in a basement with a seven-foot ceiling, it is not. Schwinn's figure for
            the 490, which has a motorised incline, is noticeably higher than for the flat 411 —
            which is the general pattern: incline adds height.
          </p>
          <p>
            Step-on height is the related figure for getting on and off. Horizon lists the EX-59's
            at 10 inches, and an independent review flags that as high for a budget machine. If
            anyone in the household has trouble stepping up, it is worth asking for this figure
            before you buy. Our guide to
            <a href="/where-to-put-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to put a treadmill</a>
            covers basements, upstairs rooms and garages, and the same reasoning applies here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-spaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Ellipticals for Small Spaces: Footprint, Folding and Power</h2>
<p>
            The best elliptical for a small space is usually a short one, not a folding one. Full-size
            home ellipticals are typically somewhere around 70 to 85 inches long once assembled; the
            compact models on this page cut that to under 60. That difference is often what decides whether a machine fits a
            spare bedroom at all.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Schwinn 411:</strong> 53.8 inches long and 24 inches wide, by Schwinn's
            figures and an independent review. The shortest machine here by a wide margin.</li>
            <li><strong>Schwinn 490:</strong> a 58-inch-long frame, which an independent review
            also notes, with a full 20-inch stride and motorised incline.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack X16:</strong> a 58.5-inch footprint, but 74 inches tall, so
            check the ceiling as well as the floor.</li>
            <li><strong>SOLE E35:</strong> 70 inches long and 31 wide. <strong>SOLE E95S:</strong>
            84 by 32 inches — the biggest machine on the page.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Remember the space around the machine. You need room to step on and off from the
            side, clearance behind the pedals at full travel, and space to reach the plug. A common
            guide is a couple of feet of clear floor around the sides and back; the owner's manual
            gives the maker's figure.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Folding ellipticals</h3>
          <p>
            Folding ellipticals exist, but among the established brands we checked they have
            largely disappeared, and we could not verify a current folding model to our standard.
            If you do buy one, look for a published folded footprint, a stride of at least 18
            inches, a frame warranty in years rather than months, and a transport-wheel design that
            one person can actually move. A folding machine that stays unfolded because folding it is
            a chore has saved no space.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power needs</h3>
          <p>
            Every pick here needs a wall socket. SOLE's and Schwinn's spec sheets say their machines
            plug into a standard household outlet, and the NordicTrack X16's touchscreen and
            motorised incline mean it does too. Horizon adds a detail worth noting: its spec sheet
            says the EX-59 is not compatible with GFCI outlets, the kind often fitted in garages,
            basements and bathrooms. If your only nearby socket is GFCI-protected, ask the retailer
            before you order. Ellipticals draw far less power than a treadmill, but the same advice
            applies about plugging directly into the wall; see our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cords</a>
            for why. Some cheaper ellipticals run without mains power at all, using a generator or
            batteries for the console; none of our picks is one of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, the floor. Full-size ellipticals are heavy, and the
            moving pedals transmit some vibration. A firm equipment mat
            protects flooring and steadies the frame on carpet; our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats guide</a>
            covers sizes and materials, and the same mats suit an elliptical.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Incline Ramps, Resistance and Noise</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> An incline ramp tilts the pedal path so the motion becomes
            more of a climb. Makers and reviewers commonly describe it as shifting more of the work
            to the glutes and hamstrings; whether it matters to you depends on whether you get bored
            of one motion. There are three kinds on the market:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>None.</strong> The Horizon EX-59 and Schwinn 411 are flat. Simpler and
            cheaper, with nothing extra to go wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual ramps,</strong> set by hand before a workout. Schwinn's 430 uses a
            six-position ramp; we could not list it because it was out of stock.</li>
            <li><strong>Powered incline,</strong> changed while you move. The SOLE E35 has 20
            levels; the Schwinn 490 runs from a 5% decline to a 15% incline; the NordicTrack X16
            from a 10% decline to a 10% incline. Decline is unusual on an elliptical and mainly
            matters if you follow app workouts that simulate routes.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One caution: SOLE's current specification table for the E95S lists no power incline,
            while an independent review of the E95S describes 20 incline levels. Those cannot both be
            right for the machine on sale today, so if incline matters to you on that model,
            confirm it with SOLE in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Resistance and flywheels.</strong> Every pick uses magnetic resistance, which is
            quiet and needs no adjustment over time. Flywheel weight is often quoted as a shorthand
            for smoothness, and a heavier flywheel does generally carry momentum better through the
            weak points of the stride. But the figure is not standardised — makers do not say whether
            they are quoting the flywheel alone or the whole rotating assembly — and design matters
            as much as mass. We list a flywheel weight only where the maker and an independent
            source agree: 25 lb on the SOLE E35 and 30 lb on the E95S. Horizon's own page for the
            EX-59 gives two different figures, so we have left it out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> A magnetic elliptical in good order is one of the quieter
            cardio machines: no motor running the whole time, no feet striking a deck. Reviewers
            describe the Horizon EX-59's magnetic resistance as quiet, and the NordicTrack X16 as
            operating smoothly and quietly. What makes noise is wear — rail wheels, pedal-arm
            bushings, a loose bolt — and the incline motor while it moves. For a flat or upstairs,
            the floor transmits more than the air: a dense mat underneath helps more than any
            choice of model. If noise is the deciding factor, our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical comparison</a>
            explains why an elliptical is usually the quieter of the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Apps and Subscriptions: What You Pay After Delivery</h2>
<p>
            Every machine on this page works without a subscription, but how much of the machine
            you can use without one varies a great deal, and that is worth knowing before you
            compare prices.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>SOLE</strong> markets its ellipticals as "no monthly membership" and offers
            its SOLE+ app free; the E35's and E95S's touchscreens can also run streaming apps you
            already pay for.</li>
            <li><strong>Horizon EX-59</strong> keeps it simple: built-in programmes, Bluetooth
            speakers, and a tablet shelf for whatever app you prefer.</li>
            <li><strong>Schwinn</strong> pairs its machines with the JRNY app. The 490 comes with a
            free trial, after which it is a paid membership; the 411 links to the Explore the World
            app, which unlocks more routes with a paid subscription. Both machines work as plain
            ellipticals without either.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack X16</strong> is built around iFIT. NordicTrack's page states that
            a paid iFIT Pro subscription and Wi-Fi are needed for the full content and features on
            the machine, and one month is included with purchase. Without it you lose most of what
            the screen is for. Our page on
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            covers what still works.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We do not quote subscription prices because they change; check the current price
            before you buy, and add a few years of it to the machine's cost when comparing a
            connected model with a plain one. A $2,300 machine with a subscription can cost more
            over its life than a $2,700 machine without one. Our comparison of
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT and Peloton</a>
            explains how the two main platforms differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            An elliptical is a good answer for low-impact cardio at home. It is not the right
            answer for everyone, and a few buyers should spend the money differently.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are training for running,</strong> an elliptical builds fitness but not
            the specific impact tolerance and rhythm that running needs. A treadmill is the better
            tool; start with our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for home</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your ceiling is low and you are tall,</strong> an exercise bike sidesteps the
            pedal-height problem entirely. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike</a>
            comparison covers what a bike does and does not replace.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want the most work in the least space,</strong> a rower folds or stands
            upright and trains the upper body harder than an elliptical's moving handles do; see
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-rowing-machine/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs rowing machine</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will only use it occasionally,</strong> be honest with yourself about the
            numbers. Our comparison of a
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home machine and a gym membership</a>
            works through the cost per session, and a gym gives you a dozen ellipticals to try.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a joint or heart condition,</strong> an elliptical is often
            described as gentle, and for many people it is. But that is general information, not
            advice for you. Talk to your doctor or physiotherapist about what kind of exercise suits
            you before you choose a machine. And if you are building out a whole room rather than
            buying one machine, our
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio equipment guide</a>
            and the <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym hub</a> are the
            places to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best elliptical machine for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, a full-size machine with a 20-inch stride, magnetic resistance and a frame warranty of ten years or more. The SOLE E35 fits that description and adds a power incline. If space or budget is tight, the Horizon EX-59 or Schwinn 411 are sensible smaller machines; if users differ a lot in height, look at an adjustable stride such as the SOLE E95S.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What stride length do I need on an elliptical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no official standard. A common rule of thumb is that 18 inches suits most users up to average height, 20 inches suits most adults, and people over about six feet often prefer 21 inches or more. Treat that as a starting point. If several people of different heights will use the machine, an adjustable stride is the safest choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ceiling height do I need for an elliptical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add your height, the machine's maximum pedal height and a few inches of headroom, then compare with the lowest point of your ceiling. Some makers publish a minimum ceiling directly; Schwinn lists its 411 as user height plus 17 inches. Machines with an incline ramp need more, because raising the ramp lifts the pedals. Basements with seven-foot ceilings often rule out taller users.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best elliptical for a small space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a short machine rather than a folding one. The Schwinn 411 is under 54 inches long by Schwinn's figures, and the Schwinn 490 fits a 20-inch stride and powered incline into a 58-inch frame. Remember to leave clear space around the machine to get on and off, and check the ceiling as well as the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a front drive or rear drive elliptical better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better in itself. Front-drive machines are the most common in homes, tend to give a slightly more uphill, climbing feel and are usually cheaper to build well. Rear-drive machines are often described as flatter and closer to walking. Centre-drive designs save length. Stride length and frame quality matter more than drive position for most buyers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do home ellipticals need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                None of the machines on this page needs one to work. SOLE offers its app free, Horizon's EX-59 has built-in programmes, and Schwinn's machines work as plain ellipticals without JRNY. NordicTrack's X16 runs without iFIT, but NordicTrack says a paid iFIT Pro subscription is needed for the full content and features. Check the current subscription price before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Home Elliptical</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most elliptical regrets come down to fit and space rather than features, and all four of these are avoidable with a tape measure and ten minutes with the spec sheet.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">People measure the floor and forget that the pedals lift you up to a foot or more off it, higher still with an incline ramp raised. A tall user in a basement can find their head at the ceiling on the first stride. Add your height, the maker's pedal height or ceiling figure and a few inches of headroom before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a short stride for a tall user</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An 18-inch stride is fine for many people and cramped for tall ones, and a short stride is the most common reason an elliptical ends up as a clothes rail. If the main user is over about six feet, or users vary a lot in height, choose a 20-inch stride at least, or an adjustable one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing flywheel weights as if they were standard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Flywheel weight is quoted as a shorthand for smoothness, but makers do not measure it the same way and design matters as much as mass. Use it as one clue among several. A machine with a confirmed 20-inch stride, a long frame warranty and consistent figures across sources is a better bet than one that wins on a single number.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the cost after delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A connected elliptical's screen may need a paid subscription to do most of what was advertised, and delivery, assembly and protection plans add up. Before comparing prices, add the subscription you will actually keep, delivery to the room you need, and the cost of a mat. The cheaper machine is sometimes the dearer one after a year.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

