        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most homes, the SOLE E35 is the elliptical to buy:</strong> a
            20-inch stride, a 350 lb rating, power incline and no subscription for about $1,700. If
            the budget stops under $1,000, the <strong class="text-white">Horizon EX-59</strong> is
            the sound simpler choice; if space is the limit, the <strong class="text-white">Schwinn
            411</strong> is the one that fits; and for households of very different heights, the
            <strong class="text-white">SOLE E95S</strong> lets everyone set their own stride.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, measure twice: the floor, the clear space around the machine, and
            the ceiling at the top of the stride. If you are still choosing between machine types,
            our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill vs elliptical comparison</a>
